Introduction 4. India emerged as world’s fifth largest economy by Nominal GDP and third largest by PPP in the year 2022[4] . Along with development of extensive tangible infrastructures there has been emphasis on change and progress at grass root level. Social building blocks and interventions in the form of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan for increasing access to education, midday meal for nutritious food to children, Mission Indra Dhanush for Routine Immunization (RI), comprehensive primary care through Health and Wellness Centers (HWCs) and other measures for ensuring maternal and child health have been implemented to ensure a community where people are healthy (not merely illness free) and take onus of their health and wellbeing. In health sector, India has made substantial progress over the past decades. It is evident from the fact that, life expectancy at birth in India has grown from 45 years in 1960s to 70 currently. [5 ] 
India is presently in a state of demographic and epidemiological transition. It bears triple burden of diseases namely, the infectious diseases; the noncommunicable diseases (NCDs) linked with lifestyle changes; and emerging epidemics and pandemics[6] . Despite making considerable progress over the years, it continues to face challenges with reproductive health, child health and nutrition[7] . Indicators, like, Maternal Mortality Ratio in India being just below 100[8] , Infant Mortality Rate being 28 and Under 5 Mortality rate being 32[9] suggest that India still has a long way to go to achieve SDG targets[10] . It is essential to understand that better health and wellbeing of individuals cannot be established only through development of curative health services. Various interrelated social and behavioral determinants of health that contribute towards morbidities and mortalities need to be taken into consideration as they pose tougher challenges to the public health system. In fact, the epidemiological transition is fueled by these social and behavioral determinants of health in addition to other risk factors. Nutrition is a crucial factor for health and development. Similarly, it is fundamental for improvement in infant, child and maternal health. It also leads to stronger immune systems and contributes to safer pregnancy and childbirth and lowers the risk of non-communicable diseases[11] . Over the years, India continues to face a double burden of nutritional problem. It can be seen through NFHS-5 data that, there is sub-optimal progress in health and nutritional status in the population. In 2019 –21, only 11% of breast-feeding children aged 6 - 23 months (about 2 years) receive an adequate diet, which was 8.3% in 2015-16 (NFHS 4). On the other hand, among the non - breastfeeding children the number has gone down to 12.7%, from 14.3% in 2015-16. When we consider children aged under 5 years, 35.5% are stunted (height-for-age), 32.1% are underweight (weight-for-age) and 19.3% are wasted (weight-for-height). Additionally, percentage of anemic children among children aged 6-59 months (about 5 years) has increased from 59% to 67% between 2015-16 to 2019-21. Malnutrition and anemia in young age sustains throughout the life span of individuals, especially women. Among the women aged between 15-19 years, 59% are anemic and among pregnant women aged 15-49 years 52.2% are anemic. On the other hand, obesity is also increasing its prevalence in India. According to NFHS – 5 data, 24% women and 23% of men in India are overweight or obese. Additionally, 57% women and 48% men have high risk waist-to-hip ratio[12] . The double burden of high prevalence of undernourishment and increasing prevalence of obesity in a country reiterates the need for social and behavior change interventions. The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), in particular, SDG 3 – Good health and well-being, and SGD 5 – Gender Equality cannot be achieved without bringing in behavioural change at all levels, combined with structural policy implementation. While practitioners of SBCC in India at state and district levels are tackling various challenges related to health (maternal, infant, and child mortality, etc.,), gender biases and equity and participatory approaches of practitioners, SBCC representatives on field and within communities, they can engage more effectively for partnerships through greater resource utilization and wider impact of SBCC interventions. The SBCC program falls within the ambit of SDG 3, which focuses on the following goals: 
- Reduce the mortality rate from noncommunicable diseases. > 4 https://www.imf.org/external/datamapper/NGDPD@WEO/IND 
