AB  - The purpose of this evaluation will be focused on supporting UNICEF and NORAD's quest for accountability and learning for key stakeholders in the response in the three countries. As such, this evaluation therefore has a formative element and a summative element: • Formative (learning): it aims to generate information that can inform the design and implementation of future UNICEF initiatives aimed at strengthening the public education system for the effective delivery of quality educational outcomes. The evaluation aims to inform future implementation strategies and the project's theory of change. It aims to highlight potential corrective actions and identify good practices and effective strategies to explore further in the future. The assessment also aims to provide advice and guidance on future strategies related to education and multi-sectoral issues such as child protection, child marriage, gender-based violence and programs focused on adolescent girls;  • Summative (accountability): It aims to hold implementing agencies, development partners and duty bearers accountable for the performance of the intervention and the results achieved in terms of strengthening systems for the effective delivery of quality education. The evaluation aims to provide solid evidence to the donor, relevant development partners and population groups, including boys and girls served by UNICEF, on the extent to which the intervention achieved its objectives in each of the countries involved. This includes both vertical accountability to the donor and horizontal accountability to the populations served.EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

1. INTRODUCTION

Following the implementation of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel in Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger (2017-2023), this independent and formative evaluation was commissioned and carried out thanks to the technical and financial support of UNICEF. This evaluation, conducted from January 2024 to October 2024, is undertaken to promote accountability and learning about the implementation of NORAD interventions in the three countries. It aims first of all to assess the achievements of the program, draw lessons learned and make recommendations to inform strategic and operational decision-making for similar future interventions. 2. PURPOSE, USE AND USERS OF THE EVALUATION

This evaluation had two main purposes. The first is formative (learning) to generate information that can inform the design and implementation of future UNICEF initiatives aimed at strengthening the effectiveness of public education systems in producing quality outcomes. The second is summative (accountability) and aims to inform donors and the community of the results obtained in terms of strengthening systems for the effective delivery of quality education. The main users of this evaluation are UNICEF, the Governments of the 3 countries (through the Ministry of Education and other sectoral ministries) and development partners in the field of education/child protection as well as the local communities/bodies served by the program evaluated. 3. OBJECTIVES OF THE EVALUATION

The general objective of this evaluation is to assess the achievements of the said program, to draw lessons learned and to make recommendations which will better inform strategic and operational decision-making with a view to improving the future implementation of similar interventions. Specifically, the evaluation aims for four (4) objectives, namely: (i) Determine the relevance, coherence, efficiency and effectiveness, impact, sustainability and gender, human rights and equity dimensions of the project; (ii) Identify good practices as well as lessons learned on what worked and what did not work in the project, including unexpected results (positive and negative); (iii) Provide advice and guidance on future strategies to be implemented/strengthened in relation to the regional education strategy, as well as multi-sectoral issues such as child protection, child marriage, GBV or convergence programs around adolescent girls; and, (iv) Formulate strategic and operational recommendations. 4. SCOPE OF THE EVALUATION

The thematic scope of this evaluation concerned four (4) components of the program: (i) Expanding access to education for the most marginalized (KRC3); (ii) Improve the quality of basic education (KRC4), (iii) Strengthen research and monitoring of the education system and (iv) Ensure communication and visibility of the project. The geographical scope of primary data collection covered the regional level through UNICEF WCARO and the central and decentralized levels of the 3 countries concerned. The evaluation. covered the period from 2017 to 2023 for Mali and Niger, taking into account addendums since 2019, and from 2017 to 2021 for Burkina Faso. 5. EVALUATION CRITERIA AND QUESTIONS

The evaluation focused on six (6) main criteria of the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG) and the Development Assistance Committee of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (DAC/OECD) namely: (i) relevance, (ii) coherence, (iii) effectiveness, (iv) efficiency, (iv) impact and (vi) sustainability. In addition to these criteria, the cross-cutting criterion of gender equality, human rights and equity was also taken into account. All of these 7 criteria retained gave rise to 7 evaluation questions and 24 evaluation sub-questions developed through an evaluation matrix. 6. EVALUATION METHODOLOGY

The evaluation methodology followed a human rights and gender-based approach with a focus on equity, in line with UNICEF ethical standards for research, evaluation, data collection and analysis. Thus, the evaluation adopted the approach of analysis of contributions as well as that of the so-called "_Most Significant Change_ (CPS)" aimed at capturing the change and evaluating the effects of the program. In addition, a quasi-experimental impact analysis based on the method of Propensity Score Matching (ASP) was used to compare the "treated" and "non-treated" groups (transversal) between schools which benefited from the program's interventions and those who have not benefited from it. The evaluation used six (6) main data collection methods: _documentary review_ with 42 documents consulted; _semi-structured individual interviews_ carried out in person or virtually at the regional level through UNICEF WCARO as well as at the central (capital) and decentralized level in the 3 countries concerned with 98 key informants (including 24 women) out of the 110 interviews 
 Page | xi 
planned (i.e. 89% participation rate); 30[1] _Focus Group Discussion (FGD)_ carried out in Mali and Burkina out of all 40 planned (i.e. 75% completed); 12 _Frame H_ sessions out of the 16 planned (75%) with groups of 6 to 8 children (girls and boys separately) under 12 years old in Burkina Faso and Mali; _an online questionnaire_ for which a total of 128[2] people for the 3 countries out of the 180 targeted respondents (i.e. 71% participation) responded favorably and completely regarding the Online Perception Survey (EPEL); _a Household Opinion Survey_ (EOAM) which reached 649[3] households in total out of the 650 targeted. Also, it should be noted that the participation of stakeholders in these focus groups and in the Cadre H sessions was done on the basis of purposive sampling which made it possible to ensure the consideration of women, vulnerable categories, including people living with disabilities, particularly 5 people in total took part in the focus groups and Cadre H sessions. 
Two analysis approaches were applied: the qualitative approach (content and comparative analysis using the Excel software package), the quantitative approach (descriptive statistical analyzes at the 5% threshold with SPSS software and impact analysis with propensity score matching to measure the impact of the different interventions of the Program evaluated with STATA software). Finally, the overall analysis of the results was based on a triangulation of data comparing at least three sources (documentary review, individual interviews/FGD/Cadre H/EPEL and EOAM). 7. LIMITS OF THE EVALUATION AND MITIGATION MEASURES IMPLEMENTED

Several limitations were identified in this evaluation. One of them concerns the reduced sample size with only 4 schools per country (2 in the treatment group and 2 in the control group) for a total of schools numbering 526 and 2,292 respectively in Mali and Burkina Faso. The use of mixed research methods and triangulation with qualitative data to complement quantitative findings helped provide depth and richer context, especially regarding the implementation process and stakeholder perceptions. The Online Perception Survey (EPEL), which informed several analyses, is based on a sample of 128 very diverse and non-representative stakeholders. Also, a limitation should be noted, the absence of basic data necessary to accurately measure the change attributed to the intervention. Therefore, the use of a quasi-experimental research method consisting of a combination of Propensity Score Matching (PSM) complemented by a variety of qualitative perspectives (KII, FGD and Frame H) and a review of program documentation allowed the identification of proxy indicators to measure the change attributed to the intervention evaluated. Finally, the difficulty of finding the implementation actors and beneficiaries of the program at all levels given the time elapsed between the implementation period and that of the evaluation and the unavailability of focal points and people identified for carrying out interviews during the period allocated to data collection should be noted. Strategies have been put in place to obtain up-to-date lists of actors and beneficiaries and to reschedule appointments, including by telephone, in virtual mode when necessary _._ 

8. MAIN FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS BY EVALUATION CRITERION

The conclusions are based on the responses to the 7 evaluation criteria assigned to this evaluation, including: 
 1. Concerning the relevance of the evaluated program, first of all, it emerges from the evaluation, the existence of a clear distinction between the needs identified during the design of the program and those emerged during its implementation relating to contextual factors linked to population movements, the COVID-19 pandemic and the change in socio-political and security context in the three countries. The interviews carried out make it possible to affirm that the activities implemented by the program evaluated were deemed adequate and in relation to the initial needs or those which emerged during the implementation of all the stakeholders, whether institutional or community type. However, this result remains to be put into perspective if we stick to the results from the EPEL with participants at regional, national and decentralized levels as well as with secondary obligation bearers (School management staff, Teachers) and holders of indirect rights (Parents and family members of students, Members of the community adjacent to the schools visited). Finally, based on two (2) main elements, the evaluation notes a strong adaptation during the implementation of the program in connection with the changing contextual factors relating to population movements, the COVID-19 pandemic and the change in socio-political and security context in the three countries studied. These 2 elements being: (i) the development and implementation of adaptive strategies (education in emergencies; strengthening the resilience of school system actors in affected areas), and (ii) the implementation of innovative and alternative educational offers in order to ensure the continuity of educational services for vulnerable groups during disruptive events with a view to taking into account the needs induced by changes occurring in the national context of the countries. > 1 Including 11 FGDs carried out with children aged 12-17 whether or not in formal schools or not; 6 FGDs with teachers and school principals; 6 FGDs carried out with members of the school management committee, 3 FGDs with the parents’ association and 4 FGDs with the association of mother-educators. > 2 This survey was carried out among 44 representatives of government structures/national institutions/sectoral administrations, 7 actors from the implementation, coordination and monitoring-evaluation bodies of the evaluated program (2017-2023) from NGOs/CSOs/national associations specializing in the protection of children and 6 actors from ASNU as well as with 70 bearers of secondary obligations and holders of indirect rights. > 3 That is, 329 households in Burkina Faso were affected (including 166 intervention-beneficiary households and 163 non-beneficiary households) while 320 households in Mali were also affected (161 intervention-beneficiary households and 159 non-beneficiary households). Page | xi 
 2. Regarding the coherence of the evaluated program, the evaluation highlights a fairly perceptible complementarity between UNICEF's interventions and those implemented by the Governments in the 3 countries to reach the most vulnerable people in the areas of education and child protection. This is manifested through Components 1 to 3 of the program and the following national programs: the Strategic Development Program for Basic Education (PDSED, 2012-2021) in Burkina Faso, the Ten-year Program for the Development of Second Generation Education and Vocational Training (PRODEC 2, 2019-2028) in Mali and the Sector Program for Education and Training (PTSE 2014-2024) in Niger. Furthermore, the evaluation also noted that the 4 fundamental humanitarian principles guided the work of all stakeholders. The design and implementation of interventions aimed to save lives, alleviate suffering, maintain human dignity and protect the rights of affected populations. 3. The analysis of the effectiveness of the evaluated Program was carried out on the basis of the program results framework which highlights contrasting results depending on the countries and program components. Two satisfactory performance indicators were noted in Niger, compared to only one in Mali and Burkina Faso. Analysis of program products shows that Component 1: Expanding access to education for the most marginalized achieved satisfactory performance for 2 out of 3 products in Mali and all 3 products in Burkina Faso. For Component 2: Improving the quality of basic education, the three products from Burkina Faso as well as products 2.1: Capacity building of members of school management committees and 2.3: Establishment and functionality of a database on target schools in Mali were judged satisfactory, while those from Niger presented insufficient performance. On the other hand, Component 3: Strengthening research and monitoring of the education system recorded unsatisfactory performance in Niger, while Burkina Faso obtained a satisfactory result for Product 3.1: Carrying out the surveys planned as part of Edu-Track. Finally, Component 5: Supporting country offices in improving access to quality education by developing effective strategic tools at the regional level was deemed satisfactory, with the establishment of a public good by UNICEF WCARO. The evaluation identified several internal and external factors that influenced the effectiveness of the program. Among the positive factors, we note the existence of financial and material resources, the adaptation capacity of stakeholders, the deployment of appropriate strategic and technical approaches, as well as the strong involvement of local communities. However, significant challenges have hampered the implementation of the program, including security constraints, the COVID-19 pandemic, insufficient human and financial resources, as well as lack of coordination with some key sector ministries. The program generated several unexpected results, both positive and negative. Among the positive effects, we observe an increased commitment of local communities in favor of education, particularly for girls, as well as the empowerment of teachers thanks to the training received. Also, the evaluation noted that in its implementation, the Program evaluated resulted in a challenge linked to the overcrowding/overload of classes and the demand for early childhood schooling (3 to 5 years) by the communities following the implementation of interventions at the level of Component 1 of the program. And this in a context where one of the challenges of the Program lay in the fact that the construction/rehabilitation of school infrastructure was not planned during its implementation. The involvement of strategic and operational partners was judged generally positive, although a lack of collaboration with key ministries limited certain initiatives, particularly in supporting professional training for young girls. Concerning the impact of COVID-19, the evaluation shows a contrasting situation: some stakeholders believe that the health crisis has had little disruption to the program, while others note a drop in the enrollment rate and a deterioration in the quality of education. Nevertheless, the program has significantly strengthened the resilience of schools in the face of the pandemic, in particular through the implementation of distance learning solutions and the distribution of health materials. Finally, the program's monitoring and evaluation system was generally effective, making it possible to adapt implementation strategies. However, certain gaps persist, particularly in Niger where the Edu-Track tool has not yet been fully implemented. In conclusion, the program has achieved several objectives despite major constraints, but improvements are needed to strengthen its effectiveness and future sustainability. 4. As part of the analysis of the efficiency of the Program evaluated, the evaluation reveals that the main stakeholders (whether governmental or NGO type) are unanimous in the provision of sufficient financial resources, but with delays considered inappropriate for the implementation of the Program. These opinions are also supported by resource expenditures estimated at $17,642,147 USD for resource mobilization beyond the expected level ($18,470,196 USD) compared to the programmable resources at the start of implementation which were $15,540,305 USD for all 3 countries. Consequently, the evaluation noted budget execution considered satisfactory (96%) despite the impacts of the COVID-19 health crisis and the persistent security instability in the three countries. Stakeholders from the 3 countries are unanimous regarding the good quality and quantity of human resources deployed in a timely manner for the implementation of interventions. However, delays and staff shortages were noted for NGO partners due to insufficient planning. Page | xiii 
Regarding the supply of material resources, particularly school and educational kits for students and teachers, was judged to be of impeccable quality, but in insufficient quantity and often delivered with significant delays. The delivery of these material resources (school/educational kits, construction materials for local colleges specifically in Niger) encountered great difficulties due to the security situation. In addition, many suppliers have not respected their commitments due to ECOWAS sanctions on financial transactions linked to the political-military situation in the three countries. 5. The analysis of the impact of the evaluated Program, based on the implementation of the interventions reveals a positive development in Niger in the perception of EPEL participants, at the regional, national and decentralized levels, concerning the gross admission rate to the last year of primary education. In Mali, this level remained constant between the start and end of program implementation. This observation is
------Lessons and Recommendations-----

224. For all 3 countries covered by the evaluated program, these lessons learned are as follows:

▪ Lesson #1: Need to further strengthen community resilience to meet children’s educational needs 
 due to the negative impact of the security crisis on the education sector as a whole, including on the program evaluated. LA1. The political crisis has aggravated the negative impact of the security crisis on the education sector. In such a context, it is necessary to reveal the need to further strengthen the resilience of communities by empowering them through the establishment of active community structures (AME, COGES) to meet the educational needs of children. ▪ Lesson No. 2: Need to design or revise a results framework while ensuring its robustness with a view to strengthening the evaluability of the underlying program. LA2. The evaluability of policies, programs and projects depends on the strength of the results framework and the availability of a functional monitoring and evaluation mechanism. The technical weaknesses of the results framework (several indicators of which lack a reference value and even a target) make it impossible to assess either the results or the induced effects of the implementation of the program. This situation reduced the evaluability of the indicators and, in turn, that of the Program being evaluated. Thus, although the program underwent several adjustments due to external factors, it was important to ensure the revision of its results framework to take these situations into account. - Lesson No. 3: Strong communication with institutional and community actors around the interventions that ensured synergy of actions between the different stakeholders. LA3. A communication strategy around the Program's interventions aimed at strategic partners and the communities served was crucial and enabled the effective involvement and buy-in of all these stakeholders in the program's vision. - Lesson No. 4: Strong involvement of institutional actors in design, training, supervision, monitoring-evaluation and coordination activities which made it possible to obtain the expected results of the implementation of Program interventions. LA4. The effective and regular involvement of actors from the ministries in charge of education allowed them to be in control and to coordinate all the activities implemented with a view to contributing positively to obtaining the expected Program results. ▪ Lesson No. 1: Need to strengthen the monitoring-evaluation framework, reporting of interventions and knowledge management

 LA1. Strengthening the monitoring-evaluation framework as well as reporting on the implementation of activities linked to the results framework and knowledge management should allow access to the necessary and timely information, with a view to strengthening the visibility of changes resulting from interventions and the accountability of the program. - Lesson No. 2: Establishment of functional community bodies, a guarantee of achieving the principle of social and behavioral change sought within communities. LA2. The establishment and operation of community bodies (AME, COGES) were levers for effective implementation and significantly contributed to achieving the social and behavioral change sought within the communities served by the program evaluated. 226. Specifically, for each country, the following lessons learned were revealed:

- Lesson No. 3: Strong positive correlation between the functionality of School Management Committees (COGES) and retention as well 
- that student performance (Burkina Faso). Page | 81 
 LA3. It was highlighted on the database of the areas served, the importance of the functionality of COGES on the learning and retention of students revealed by the existence of a direct correlation between the functionality of COGES and the abandonment of studies by students and the existence of additional chances of obtaining passing grades. ▪ Lesson No. 4: Strong positive correlation between teacher training and the functionality of School Management Committees

▪ Lesson No. 5: Need to strengthen the involvement of partners at the decentralized level to ensure optimal implementation of interventions and support for the targeted objectives (Niger). LA5. Decisions and agreements reached at the central level are often not translated into the operational functioning of government implementing partners at decentralized levels. It therefore proved necessary to strengthen the decentralization of public services in order to ensure the design of educational programs directed by stakeholders at this level, including municipalities in particular. 10. RECOMMENDATIONS

The evaluation of the “Program to improve access to quality education for children in the Sahel (2017-2023)” made it possible to identify strategic and operational recommendations essential for improving future interventions. These recommendations, resulting from a participatory process involving stakeholders (governments, UNICEF, NORAD), are classified according to their priority and the resources required for their implementation. CHAPTER 10. RECOMMENDATIONS

 229. Based on a participatory and inclusive process with the participation of stakeholders (bond holders and GRE), this evaluation made it possible to identify nine (9) recommendations, including five (5) of a strategic nature and four (4) of an operational nature. These call for direct action by Governments, MEN, UNICEF and NORAD to improve possible future interventions. These recommendations are classified according to their level of priority (High/Medium/Low) and the level of resources (financial, human, material) necessary for their implementation (High/Medium/Low). 10.1. Strategic Recommendations

